/target/
# Alternate CARGO_TARGET_DIRs (glibc builds, sandboxed or parallel agent runs).
# One pattern rather than one line per directory: these get created ad hoc, and
# an unignored one is several GB of build artifacts staged into a commit.
/target-*/
**/*.rs.bk
# Bytecode from the repo's python helpers (`contrib/find-unwired.py`).
__pycache__/
*.pdb
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
.env
# demo screenshots + mp4 (regenerated by vhs; only demo.gif is committed)
demo/frame-*.png
demo/demo.mp4
# per-project wizard runtime state (checkpoints, etc.)
.wizard/
# generate_image tool output
/generated/
# working notes and plans, not part of the shipped repo
/internal/

# Scratch git worktrees created for background agents.
.claude/worktrees/

# Secrets, in the one place nothing else stops them. Runtime credentials live
# under $HOME/.wizard/ and never come near a checkout, so this is not about
# them; it is about the keys that get written here by hand. The concrete one:
# `contrib/seed-release-key.sh` takes the keyfile as an optional argument, so
# `contrib/seed-release-key.sh ./release.key` puts the *release signing private
# key* in the repository root, and from there a `git add -A` is the whole
# accident. That key signs every release `wizard update` will ever accept, and a
# commit is not a place it can be taken back from.
#
# Patterns, not filenames, because the argument is whatever the caller typed.
# Nothing tracked today matches any of them (checked with `git ls-files`); if a
# fixture ever needs to, negate it on its own line rather than dropping a
# pattern — `git check-ignore -v <path>` names the line that caught a file.
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
credentials.toml
*_oauth.json
